Attributions & prior art
========================

This project is original code, built on publicly documented hardware
protocols and the research of others:

- Linux kernel `drivers/input/joystick/xpad.c` (GPLv2) — the authoritative
  public reference for the Xbox 360 wireless receiver wire protocol
  (interface signature FF/5D/81, presence/input packet layout, LED/rumble
  command formats). No xpad code is copied; protocol facts only.
  https://github.com/torvalds/linux

- @nondebug "dualsense" protocol documentation — Sony DualSense USB HID
  report descriptor (273 bytes, extracted to src/ds_desc.h) and input/output
  report layouts. Hardware interface facts.
  https://github.com/nondebug/dualsense

- nabekhan/X360MacOSReceiverBridge — research-grade proof that the
  receiver can be driven from macOS user space via libusb and that
  IOHIDUserDevice is gated by com.apple.developer.hid.virtual.device.
  Its docs/RESEARCH.md informed the architecture.
  https://github.com/nabekhan/X360MacOSReceiverBridge

- trollzem/Lumen — documented the community-standard AMFI relaxation flow
  (nvram boot-args="amfi_get_out_of_my_way=1") that makes ad-hoc-signed
  virtual HID possible on modern macOS.
  https://github.com/trollzem/Lumen

- opensource-apple/IOKitUser (Apple open source) — IOHIDUserDevice.h
  callback signatures (IOHIDUserDeviceRegisterSetReportCallback etc.).
  https://github.com/opensource-apple/IOKitUser

- 360Controller/360Controller (GPL) — the original kext driver this project
  succeeds. Its release notes document why a rewrite was necessary.
  https://github.com/360Controller/360Controller

- rlgerma/xenon360, ThallesP/x360w-userspace — independent userspace
  prototypes confirming the approach for wired controllers / receivers.
